  "Number": 9,
  "Question": "A farmer divides his herd of \\(n\\) cows among his four sons so that one son gets one-half the herd, a second son one-fourth, a third son one-fifth, and the fourth son \\(7\\) cows. Then \\(n\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(80\\)",
    "B": "\\(100\\)",
    "C": "\\(140\\)",
    "D": "\\(180\\)",
    "E": "\\(240\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "All three stated fractions refer to the whole herd. Together, the first three sons receive\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15\n=\\frac{10+5+4}{20}\n=\\frac{19}{20}.\n\\]\nThe fourth son therefore receives \\(1-\\frac{19}{20}=\\frac1{20}\\) of the herd. Since that share is \\(7\\) cows, the whole herd has\n\\[\nn=7\\div\\frac1{20}=7\\cdot20=140\n\\]\ncows. The resulting shares are \\(70,35,28,7\\), all whole numbers totaling \\(140\\). The answer is \\(140\\), choice C.",
